# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cosmos Pay — payments microservice
# This service is NOT meant to be exposed directly to the internet.
# All traffic must arrive through the APISIX gateway (always enforced — no opt-out).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# App
# Set NODE_ENV=production in production. Two protections key on it: a request
# missing X-Plan-Swap-Fee-Bps is a 503 only in production (anywhere else swaps
# silently fall back to STELLAR_SWAP_FEE_BPS), and /docs — which sits outside
# every guard — is off by default only in production.
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3000

# Which dotenv file this process reads (default .env). Two replicas from one checkout:
# copy .env to .env.b, change PORT (and WALLET_AUTH_PUBLIC_BASE_URL if the callback
# should land on this replica), then `npm run dev:replica` — or set ENV_FILE=.env.b
# before any npm script. Same DATABASE_URL and secrets: nothing is per-process state.
# ENV_FILE=.env

# PostgreSQL connection used by Prisma (payments DB — independent from the dev platform DB)
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/cosmos_payments?schema=public

# APISIX gateway validation (always enforced). REQUIRED — must equal the dev platform's
# COSMOS_GATEWAY_SECRET (APISIX injects it as X-Gateway-Secret). `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# Minimum 32 characters — this value is the whole boundary between "arrived
# through APISIX" and "anyone who can reach the pod". Generate one with
# openssl rand -hex 32
# Left empty on purpose so the service refuses to boot until you set it. A
# placeholder is refused too: this file is public, so any value copied from it
# is a secret everyone already has.
APISIX_GATEWAY_SECRET=

# Optional APISIX header overrides (defaults shown). Lowercase values are applied at runtime.
# APISIX_GATEWAY_SECRET_HEADER=x-gateway-secret
# APISIX_CONSUMER_HEADER=x-consumer-username
# APISIX_CREDENTIAL_HEADER=x-credential-identifier
# APISIX_ENVIRONMENT_HEADER=x-consumer-env
# APISIX_ROLE_HEADER=x-consumer-role
# APISIX_PERMISSIONS_HEADER=x-consumer-permissions
# APISIX_ORGANIZATION_HEADER=x-consumer-org
# APISIX_PLAN_HEADER=x-consumer-plan
# APISIX_SWAP_FEE_BPS_HEADER=x-plan-swap-fee-bps
# Verified email of the account that owns the key. The dev platform's forwarder sets it
# and strips any client copy. Parsed onto the consumer; nothing depends on it today.
# APISIX_EMAIL_HEADER=x-consumer-email

# APISIX username of the SHARED PUBLIC consumer — the one API key embedded in every
# copy of the open-source wallet, so that people can swap without an account (at the
# public 1.5% commission; a registered org's plan rate is lower).
#
# Set it wherever a public key is published. PublicKeyGuard confines that consumer to
# the handlers marked @AllowPublicKey — quotes, envelope builders, on-chain reads and
# telemetry ingest — and refuses it everywhere else, because every anonymous caller
# arrives as this same consumer and the list endpoints filter by exactly that. Leaving
# it unset means the guard falls back to the forwarded X-Consumer-Role alone, so a
# gateway that stops sending roles would expose one anonymous user's swap history to
# the next. Belt and braces: set both.
#
# Must match the consumer the dev platform provisions for the public key.
# APISIX_PUBLIC_CONSUMER=cosmos_public

# The shared public key itself, served keyless at GET /v1/public-key?env=dev|prod
# to wallets that have no account yet. Copy the values the dev platform minted.
# Rotating = changing these; wallets pick the new one up within 5 minutes. Unset
# answers 503 for that environment, and a wallet with no account falls back to
# the key compiled into its build. The APISIX route for /v1/public-key must run
# WITHOUT key-auth.
# PUBLIC_API_KEY_DEV=dv_...
# PUBLIC_API_KEY_PROD=prod_...

# Stellar (payment intents). Fallback network when the gateway didn't forward a key env.
STELLAR_NETWORK=testnet
# Optional Horizon overrides (defaults: horizon.stellar.org / horizon-testnet.stellar.org)
# STELLAR_HORIZON_URL_PUBLIC=https://horizon.stellar.org
# STELLAR_HORIZON_URL_TESTNET=https://horizon-testnet.stellar.org
STELLAR_BASE_FEE=100
STELLAR_TX_TIMEOUT=300

# Solana and Monad (payment intents, wallet sign-in, aliases). The network tier
# follows the API key exactly as on Stellar: `prod` keys → Solana mainnet-beta /
# Monad mainnet (chain id 143), `dev` keys → Solana devnet / Monad testnet (10143).
# Unset uses the public RPCs, which are rate-limited hard — fine for a trial, not
# for production: a throttled RPC is a payment the observer does not see and a
# `POST /pay` that answers 503. Each node is checked before use (Solana genesis
# hash, Monad eth_chainId); a URL on the wrong network answers 503 misconfigured.
# SOLANA_RPC_URL_MAINNET=https://api.mainnet-beta.solana.com
# SOLANA_RPC_URL_DEVNET=https://api.devnet.solana.com
# SOLANA_RPC_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# MONAD_RPC_URL_MAINNET=https://rpc.monad.xyz
# MONAD_RPC_URL_TESTNET=https://testnet-rpc.monad.xyz
# MONAD_RPC_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# Blocks one eth_getLogs may span: your provider's limit. The public RPC refuses
# more than 100; a lower value than the provider allows only makes the observer
# catch up more slowly.
# MONAD_LOG_BLOCK_RANGE=100

# Monad deposit addresses. Set a relayer key and every Monad payment link pays
# its own CREATE2 address (contracts/PaymentForwarder.sol) instead of the
# merchant: the payment is recognised by where it went — native MON included —
# and the relayer forwards it to the merchant, less a fee fixed when the intent
# is created. Unset, Monad links pay the merchant directly and are matched by
# destination + exact amount (native MON then only settles via /validate).
# The key holds GAS MONEY ONLY: the forwarders it deploys can pay nobody but the
# merchant baked into their address (and it its fee). Fund it modestly and alert
# on its balance — an empty relayer means deposits wait, not that money is lost.
# 32-byte hex secret key.
# MONAD_RELAYER_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Relayer fee per ERC-20 deposit, in token units: {"0xToken...": "0.05"}. A
# token with no entry is forwarded free — the relayer pays the gas. Native MON's
# fee is quoted from the gas price and needs no entry.
# MONAD_DEPOSIT_TOKEN_FEES=

# Stellar native swaps (path-payment asset exchange). Non-custodial: the service
# only quotes, builds the unsigned tx and submits the signed one. The fee is taken
# in the source asset and paid to STELLAR_SWAP_FEE_WALLET. Leave the wallet blank
# only when STELLAR_SWAP_FEE_BPS=0 (fee disabled).
#
# STELLAR_SWAP_FEE_BPS is the LOCAL-DEVELOPMENT rate only. In production the rate
# is the organization's plan rate, injected per request by APISIX as
# X-Plan-Swap-Fee-Bps. When NODE_ENV=production and that header is missing, swaps
# now return 503 `misconfigured` instead of silently falling back to this value —
# a gateway that stops forwarding it used to reprice every organization with no
# alert. Make sure the APISIX consumer plugin injects the header (and that the
# route's proxy-rewrite `remove` list strips any client-supplied copy — see the
# "APISIX route configuration" section of the README) before deploying.
STELLAR_SWAP_FEE_WALLET=GARMB7W3FCR3GKIM3FLWVJASC2PUZ4VHUJZTNJVWWKNTCJNKO6TBCT76
STELLAR_SWAP_FEE_BPS=50
STELLAR_SWAP_SLIPPAGE_BPS=50
STELLAR_SWAP_MAX_SLIPPAGE_BPS=500
# true: refuse (409 operation_in_flight) a second swap from a source account that
# still has a non-expired PENDING one — for integrators that double-submit.
STELLAR_SWAP_SINGLE_INFLIGHT=false

# Cross-chain swaps (Stellar <-> Solana <-> Monad), settled by NEAR Intents 1Click.
# Mainnet only: a dev key can quote, but POST /v1/cross-chain-swaps is 400
# network_unsupported. Stellar -> Stellar stays on /v1/swaps (the native DEX).
# NEAR_INTENTS_BASE_URL=https://1click.chaindefuser.com
# Partner key (X-API-Key). Without it 1Click adds a 0.2% fee of its own and takes
# half of the commission below.
NEAR_INTENTS_API_KEY=
# NEAR account the plan commission is paid to (1Click appFees); it accrues inside
# NEAR Intents, withdraw it from there. Unset while a plan charges a commission:
# every cross-chain quote answers 503 misconfigured instead of swapping for free.
NEAR_INTENTS_FEE_RECIPIENT=
# NEAR_INTENTS_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
# Default / maximum slippage (bps); below the minimum NEAR Intents refunds.
# CROSS_CHAIN_SWAP_SLIPPAGE_BPS=100
# CROSS_CHAIN_SWAP_MAX_SLIPPAGE_BPS=500
# How long a deposit address accepts the deposit; later ones are refunded.
# CROSS_CHAIN_SWAP_DEADLINE_SECONDS=1800

# Same-chain swaps on Solana (Jupiter) and Monad (Kuru Flow): /v1/swaps with
# `chain`. Mainnet only. The commission is taken from the output and paid to:
# - SOLANA_SWAP_FEE_WALLET: the owner of the token accounts it lands in — one per
#   output mint, which must exist first (a missing one answers 503 misconfigured
#   naming the account to create).
# - MONAD_SWAP_FEE_WALLET: an EVM address.
# Unset while a plan charges a commission: that chain's swaps answer 503.
SOLANA_SWAP_FEE_WALLET=
MONAD_SWAP_FEE_WALLET=
# Jupiter's keyless tier by default; with a key use https://api.jup.ag/swap/v1.
# JUPITER_BASE_URL=https://lite-api.jup.ag/swap/v1
# JUPITER_API_KEY=
# JUPITER_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# Without KURU_API_KEY every address gets a token limited to one request a
# second: enough to try it, not for production.
# KURU_BASE_URL=https://ws.kuru.io
KURU_API_KEY=
# KURU_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# Observer (on-chain reconciler) + payment intent lifetime
OBSERVER_ENABLED=true
OBSERVER_INTERVAL_MS=15000
OBSERVER_BATCH_SIZE=50
PAYMENT_INTENT_TTL_SECONDS=3600

# OpenAPI / Swagger. /docs and /docs/json are Express middleware — NOT behind Nest guards.
# Default: enabled when NODE_ENV !== production. Set SWAGGER_ENABLED=true to opt in on prod.
# SWAGGER_ENABLED=true
# OPENAPI_SERVER_URL=https://gateway.example.com
# Request log retention (API logs view). Rows older than RETENTION_DAYS are
# deleted in bounded batches. Set REQUEST_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=0 to disable prune.
# Each cycle loops deleteMany(batch) until drained or MAX_PER_CYCLE is hit.
REQUEST_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=30
REQUEST_LOG_PRUNE_INTERVAL_MS=3600000
REQUEST_LOG_PRUNE_BATCH_SIZE=1000
REQUEST_LOG_PRUNE_MAX_PER_CYCLE=50000

# Client activity retention (the wallet/dashboard telemetry feed), on the same
# timer and with the same bounded batches. An activity row holds an IP, a user
# agent and whatever the client attached as `props`, so it is personal data on
# the same footing as the access log above. 0 keeps events forever.
ACTIVITY_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Webhook delivery body retention, on the same timer. A delivery row records what
# was sent; for RECEIVER_UPDATED that body is the provider's full KYC dossier
# (tax id, address, document links), and it is never returned by the API. After
# this many days the body of a SUCCEEDED/FAILED delivery is replaced with a
# redaction marker — the audit row stays, the dossier does not. Retryable
# deliveries are never touched: the signature covers the body, so a retry has to
# re-send it byte for byte. 0 keeps bodies forever.
WEBHOOK_PAYLOAD_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Webhook delivery sweeper. The dispatcher retries in-process, blocking between
# attempts, so a pod killed mid-backoff used to strand a PENDING delivery that
# nothing ever looked at again — a settled payment that notified nobody. The
# sweeper picks those up, plus FAILED rows still inside their retry budget.
# Default on (a lost settlement notification is not an opt-in problem); set
# WEBHOOK_SWEEP_ENABLED=false as the incident switch, e.g. to stop redelivery to
# an integrator that is melting down. Only one replica sweeps per tick — the
# timers take a Postgres advisory lock.
WEBHOOK_SWEEP_ENABLED=true
WEBHOOK_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS=60000

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BlindPay — fiat <-> stablecoin rails (onramp / offramp / KYC)
# The `blindpay` native plugin: these are read only when PLUGINS_ENABLED lists
# `blindpay` — without it the routes do not exist, whatever is set here, and the
# boot warns that the keys are unused. One platform instance shared by all
# consumers; with the plugin on and the key blank, BlindPay routes return 503.
# Get these from the BlindPay dashboard: Instance → API Keys (`in_...`).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
BLINDPAY_API_KEY=
BLINDPAY_INSTANCE_ID=
BLINDPAY_BASE_URL=https://api.blindpay.com/v1
# Svix signing secret (whsec_...) of the webhook endpoint you point at
# <gateway>/v1/blindpay/webhooks. Required when BLINDPAY_API_KEY is set.
# Paste it whole: boot fails if the part after whsec_ is not base64 decoding to
# at least 24 bytes, because a truncated secret is a key anyone can sign with.
BLINDPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
BLINDPAY_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# DeFindex vault API — the `defindex` native plugin, served only when
# PLUGINS_ENABLED lists `defindex`. With it on and the key empty, the yield
# routes answer 503 misconfigured.
DEFINDEX_API_KEY=
DEFINDEX_BASE_URL=https://api.defindex.io
DEFINDEX_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# Plugins this deployment serves, comma-separated: slugs of folders in plugins/
# (e.g. `example`, preinstalled) and the native `blindpay` and `defindex`. Empty
# serves none, and a plugin not listed is never even read — a deployment that
# used BlindPay or DeFindex before they became plugins must add them here, or
# their routes disappear. Each tenant still has to install a plugin (and consent to what
# it may touch) before it runs for them.
PLUGINS_ENABLED=
# Seals the secret settings of plugin installations (a tenant's API key for the
# plugin's own service). Required, at least 32 characters, as soon as an enabled
# plugin declares a secret setting — the boot fails without it. Changing it makes
# every stored plugin secret unreadable: tenants have to enter them again.
# PLUGINS_SECRET=
# Signers whose plugins this deployment runs, BESIDES Cosmos Pay support (always
# trusted): <keyId>:<base64url public key>, comma-separated, as
# `npm run plugins -- keygen` prints. A plugin signed by anyone else — or whose
# plugin.json or index.ts changed after signing — stops the boot.
PLUGINS_TRUSTED_KEYS=
# Run plugins that have no signature.json. For writing one locally ONLY: the boot
# refuses it when NODE_ENV=production.
PLUGINS_ALLOW_UNSIGNED=false
# Read by the CLI only (`npm run plugins -- install`), not by the service: the
# HTTPS base URL of the plugin registry to install from.
PLUGINS_REGISTRY_URL=
# The development instance, served to `dev` API keys (the variables above serve
# `prod` keys). Use a BlindPay development instance here. Leave it blank and dev
# keys get 503 `misconfigured` from every BlindPay route — they never fall back to
# the production instance, whose receivers are real identities.
# Same rules as above: instance id and webhook secret are required with the key.
BLINDPAY_API_KEY_DEV=
BLINDPAY_INSTANCE_ID_DEV=
BLINDPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET_DEV=

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Outbound webhooks (integrator notifications)
# Destinations must be public https; see README "Outbound destination policy".
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
WEBHOOK_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS=3000
WEBHOOK_READ_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
WEBHOOK_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES=65536
WEBHOOK_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
WEBHOOK_BACKOFF_MS=2000
# WEBHOOK_SIGNATURE_HEADER=x-cosmos-signature
# Optional legacy single timeout (used as fallback for connect/read if unset)
# WEBHOOK_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Per-consumer KYC redirect_url host allow-list (issue #33).
# JSON object: {"apisix_consumer_username":["allowed.example.com"]}
# Missing/empty => every consumer fails closed until configured.
# KYC_REDIRECT_URL_WHITELIST={"cosmos_acme":["acme.com","app.acme.com"]}

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Rate limiting (per client address)
#
# Opt-in per route via @RateLimit, on the routes whose cost an error cannot refund —
# XLM spent, an account created on-chain, mail sent to an address the caller chose —
# so an uncapped loop is not a way for a stranger to spend your money.
#
# The counter lives in Postgres, not in each replica's memory — an in-process
# limiter would give every replica the full budget and multiply the effective
# limit by the replica count.
#
# The address is taken from req.ip, which with the service's `trust proxy = 1`
# is the entry APISIX appended to X-Forwarded-For. A client can prepend to that
# header but not append, so the key cannot be spoofed. Do NOT raise trust proxy.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
# How often rolled-over counter windows are deleted (min 1000).
RATE_LIMIT_PRUNE_INTERVAL_MS=600000

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WALLET SIGN-IN  (/v1/wallet/auth/*, PUT /v1/wallet/backup,
#                  POST /v1/wallet/recovery/setup)
#
# The wallet's own sign-in: Authentik (OpenID Connect), Google, GitHub or an
# emailed code. It proves WHO someone is and never touches a key — the seed is
# generated on the device and sealed under the person's password, and this
# service only keeps the sealed box. Leave every variable below unset and the
# routes still answer: they report no providers and no email door, which is the
# honest state.
#
# Two layers, and neither replaces the other: APISIX key-auth proves the caller
# is a wallet talking through the gateway (the shared public key); Authentik —
# or the inbox — proves the PERSON.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The public origin a BROWSER reaches this service on — the gateway's, not the
# upstream's. The OAuth redirect URI is built from it as
#   {WALLET_AUTH_PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/v1/wallet/auth/oauth/callback/{provider}
# and that exact string must be registered with Google and GitHub. A provider
# refuses a redirect URI it does not hold verbatim, so a wrong value here fails
# at the consent screen rather than quietly in this process.
#
# This path must NOT sit behind APISIX key-auth: it is
# a browser navigation and carries no key. It is declared `@Public()` here, and
# the gateway needs a route for it with key-auth off.
WALLET_AUTH_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://gateway.example.com/cosmos-api

# Authentik — the preferred door. Create an OAuth2/OpenID provider (confidential
# client, "Authorization Code") whose redirect URI is
#   {WALLET_AUTH_PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/v1/wallet/auth/oauth/callback/authentik
# and put Google / GitHub behind it as Authentik SOURCES: this service then holds
# no Google or GitHub secret at all, and passwords, MFA and account linking live
# in Authentik. The ID token is verified against the provider's published keys
# with this service's own PKCE and nonce; `email_verified` must be true.
#
# !! READ THIS BEFORE TURNING IT ON !! Authentik's DEFAULT "email" scope mapping
# answers `email_verified: True` for EVERY user, whether or not anyone ever
# proved that address. This service and both recovery servers trust that claim —
# it is the whole of what makes an ID token a proof of an inbox. So either:
#   - make every enrollment path verify the email (an Email stage in the
#     enrollment flow; social sources only from providers that verify), or
#   - replace the mapping with one that reports the truth, e.g.
#       return {"email": request.user.email,
#               "email_verified": request.user.attributes.get("email_verified", False)}
#     and set that attribute from the verification stage.
# With neither, anyone who can enroll under someone else's address can sign in
# as them and recover their wallet.
#
# Google / GitHub as Authentik SOURCES: set the flow the sources log in through
# (default-source-authentication) to "Authentication: No requirement". Its
# default, "require unauthenticated", refuses anyone who still has an Authentik
# session, and the wallet asks for a login no older than 5 minutes (`max_age`),
# so a person re-logging in with an older session gets "Flow does not apply to
# current user" instead of a login. The flow only logs in the user already
# linked to that Google/GitHub account, so relaxing it grants nothing else.
#
# ISSUER is the provider's issuer EXACTLY as its discovery document states it —
# for Authentik that ends in a slash:
#   https://auth.example.com/application/o/<slug>/
#
# Its ID token is also what the SEP-30 recovery servers accept (RECOVERY_OIDC_*
# below), so its CLIENT_ID goes in their RECOVERY_OIDC_AUDIENCES. Set the
# provider's token validity to at least 15 minutes: a recovery hands the token
# over only after an emailed code, which takes a person a few minutes.
#
# Security keys / passkeys (WebAuthn) in Authentik only work when the person
# reaches Authentik on a HOSTNAME over https with a certificate their browser
# trusts. A browser refuses WebAuthn on an IP address (192.168.x.x), on plain
# http anywhere but localhost, and on a page with a certificate warning — so a
# phone on the LAN pointed at the dev machine's IP never offers the key. The key
# is also only asked for once it is enrolled (user settings → MFA devices), and
# offered at the username prompt only when the identification stage has its
# passkey (WebAuthn) stage set. deploy/authentik/wallet-sign-in.yaml
# sets both up and keeps every second factor OPTIONAL: whoever has none is
# offered a security key or an authenticator app after the password, never
# required to add one, and the wallet links to Authentik's MFA settings page
# from its own settings. It also puts Google / GitHub on the sign-up page, above
# the password form.
# WALLET_AUTH_OIDC_ISSUER=
# WALLET_AUTH_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
# WALLET_AUTH_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The app URLs the sign-in callback may send the browser back to, comma-
# separated. A native wallet opens the sign-in in the platform's auth session
# (ASWebAuthenticationSession, an Android Custom Tab, the system browser from a
# desktop app) and passes one of these as `returnTo`; the callback then answers
# 302 to it with ?state=… instead of rendering "go back to your wallet", which is
# what closes the session. Only the state travels: the verifier still redeems.
#   - a custom scheme the app registers:        cosmoswallet://auth/done
#   - a universal / app link the app owns:      https://wallet.example.com/auth/done
#   - a desktop loopback listener (any port):   http://127.0.0.1/auth/done
# Exact match on scheme, host and path. Refused at boot: plain http off
# 127.0.0.1 / [::1], a query or fragment, credentials, javascript:/data:/file:.
# Unset: every callback renders the page, and a wallet that sends `returnTo`
# gets 400 wallet_return_url_not_allowed.
# WALLET_AUTH_RETURN_URLS=cosmoswallet://auth/done,http://127.0.0.1/auth/done

# Direct OAuth credentials, for a self-hosted deployment with no Authentik. An
# empty pair disables that provider, and GET /v1/wallet/auth/providers reports
# what is actually available so a wallet renders the buttons that work.
# WALLET_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# WALLET_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# WALLET_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# WALLET_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The two legs that finish a sign-in, owned by this service so the developer
# platform is not in the path of any wallet request (it used to be, and every
# sign-in failed whenever it was down).
#
# 1. The sender for the sign-in code (and a recovery server's codes): MAIL_FROM
#    plus ONE transport. Resend (HTTPS on 443) wins when set — prefer it where the
#    host blocks outbound SMTP ports (OVH does); otherwise SMTP. MAIL_FROM without a
#    transport, or a transport without MAIL_FROM, refuses to boot.
#    Unset: GET /v1/wallet/auth/providers reports no email door.
# MAIL_FROM=Cosmos Pay <no-reply@example.com>
# MAIL_RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# MAIL_SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# 587 = STARTTLS (MAIL_SMTP_SECURE=false); 465 = implicit TLS (true).
# MAIL_SMTP_PORT=587
# MAIL_SMTP_SECURE=false
# MAIL_SMTP_USER=
# MAIL_SMTP_PASS=
# MAIL_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
#
# 2. The APISIX Admin API, to mint the account's dev + prod keys under the
#    consumer cosmos_wallet_<accountId>. Set both or neither. Unset: a sign-in
#    completes its callback and then answers 503 misconfigured at /finish.
#    SECURITY: the admin key is gateway-wide — APISIX has nothing narrower. The
#    client here only ever writes cosmos_wallet_* consumers, but treat this key
#    like APISIX_GATEWAY_SECRET, reach the admin API from these pods only, and
#    never set it on a recovery server (boot refuses).
# APISIX_ADMIN_URL=http://apisix:9180/apisix/admin
# APISIX_ADMIN_KEY=
# APISIX_ADMIN_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# Swap commission (bps) baked into wallet accounts' keys — the community plan's.
# WALLET_KEY_SWAP_FEE_BPS=150

# Seals the short-lived session token a finished sign-in hands the wallet — the
# only credential that can create an account or store a backup. REQUIRED as soon
# as any door above is configured, at least 32 characters, and never equal to
# APISIX_GATEWAY_SECRET (boot refuses both). It used to default to the gateway
# secret, which the developer platform also holds; a platform that could forge a
# sign-in here is not what a self-hostable server should ship with.
# WALLET_AUTH_SESSION_SECRET=

# Seals every stored wallet backup AT REST, under a key that lives here and never in
# the database: a dump, replica or backup file of the DB then holds only ciphertext of
# the device's ciphertext. REQUIRED as soon as any sign-in door is configured; 32 bytes,
# base64 or hex (openssl rand -base64 32); never equal to another secret here. Back it
# up like a key: lose it and the stored backups cannot be served again.
# Rotation: move the old value to WALLET_BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_PREVIOUS_KEYS (comma-
# separated, read-only), set a new one, run `npm run backups:reencrypt`, then drop the
# old one. The same script seals rows written before this existed.
# WALLET_BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# WALLET_BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_PREVIOUS_KEYS=

# The Horizon that says who may sign for an account. Read only when a signature
# does not come from the address's own key: a wallet RECOVERED through SEP-30
# signs with the key that replaced its master. One Horizon, the operator's —
# never one the caller names. Defaults to the public network's.
# WALLET_AUTH_SIGNERS_HORIZON_URL=https://horizon.stellar.org

# The operator pays the reserve of an account's two recovery signers
# (POST /v1/wallet/recovery/setup), once per account. Unset disables it. Must NOT
# be set on a recovery server (RECOVERY_ROLE): boot refuses the combination.
# WALLET_RECOVERY_SPONSOR_SECRET=
# WALLET_RECOVERY_SPONSOR_NETWORK_PASSPHRASE=Public Global Stellar Network ; September 2015
# WALLET_RECOVERY_SPONSOR_HORIZON_URL=https://horizon.stellar.org

# Upstream budget for one call to Google or GitHub.
# WALLET_AUTH_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# Retires handshakes and codes nobody finished. Default on: an AUTHORIZED
# handshake left in the table is a redeemable sign-in sitting there.
# WALLET_AUTH_SWEEP_ENABLED=true
# WALLET_AUTH_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS=60000

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ACCOUNT RECOVERY — SEP-10 + SEP-30  (/.well-known/stellar.toml,
#                                      /v1/sep10/auth, /v1/sep30/*)
#
# This deployment as ONE of the two recovery servers. Unset RECOVERY_ROLE and
# every route answers 404 — the normal state for the main API.
#
# Run it TWICE: role `a` and role `b`, each with its own keys, JWT secret, host
# and database, and ideally its own operator. Two shares held by one server are
# one share. Replicas of one role behind APISIX's load balancer are fine: nothing
# here is per-process state (rate limits and spent tokens live in Postgres).
#
# The routes are keyless by design — SEP-10/SEP-30 are called by wallets that
# hold no API key of ours — so APISIX must serve these prefixes WITHOUT key-auth
# and WITHOUT its CORS plugin (this service answers CORS itself, and two
# Access-Control-Allow-Origin headers make a browser refuse the response). The
# developer platform's route sync creates that route.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RECOVERY_ROLE=a

# The https origin plus gateway entry clients reach THIS server on. The TOML's
# WEB_AUTH_ENDPOINT and SEP-30 ENDPOINT are built from it, and its host is the
# web_auth_domain every challenge names.
# RECOVERY_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://recovery-a.example.com/cosmos-api

# The WALLET's domain, named by every challenge. The SAME value on both servers:
# the wallet requires the two to agree, and whoever controls one cannot change
# what the other says.
# RECOVERY_HOME_DOMAIN=example.com

# RECOVERY_NETWORK_PASSPHRASE=Public Global Stellar Network ; September 2015
# RECOVERY_HORIZON_URL=https://horizon.stellar.org

# Derives this server's on-chain signer for each account (HKDF). Back it up like
# the money it guards and NEVER rotate it while an account names it: lose it and
# every registered account keeps a signer nobody can produce. Moving a role here
# from the developer platform? Reuse its RECOVERY_SIGNER_MASTER unchanged.
# RECOVERY_SIGNER_MASTER=S...

# Signs SEP-10 challenges; its public key is published as SIGNING_KEY. Must
# differ from the master above.
# RECOVERY_SEP10_SIGNING_SECRET=S...

# Signs the SEP-10 and identity tokens. 32+ characters, its own value.
# RECOVERY_JWT_SECRET=

# How someone who lost their device proves their inbox HERE. At least one.
#
# 1. An OIDC ID token (preferred): Authentik's, verified against its keys.
#    AUDIENCES is the wallet sign-in's client id (WALLET_AUTH_OIDC_CLIENT_ID on
#    the main deployment), comma-separated if several. Each token is accepted
#    once per server and must stand for a login in the last 15 minutes.
# RECOVERY_OIDC_ISSUER=https://auth.example.com/application/o/wallet/
# RECOVERY_OIDC_AUDIENCES=
#
# 2. This server's own emailed code, sent through its own MAIL_FROM + transport
#    (required with this). Only as independent as the mail path: give
#    each role its own Resend account, since whoever delivers both servers' codes
#    sees both.
# RECOVERY_EMAIL_CODES=true

# RECOVERY_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# RECOVERY_SWEEP_ENABLED=true
# RECOVERY_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS=60000
